      "content": "Newton's third law plays a key role in the interaction with the air. On the surface of the wing, there are tiny air masses being pushed in both forward (away from where the air is being pushed) and downward (down the airflow direction), as well as the airflow particles themselves being deflected downward by the wing. This \"tucked-in\" airflow directly affects the air's motion relative to the air above the wing, further compressing and accelerating the air downward, where Bernoulli's principle comes into effect, thereby further supporting lift.",
